  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 108 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nTonight\n\nLight winds near the surface underneath a subsidence inversion \nin southwest and central Wisconsin will result in the\ndevelopment of fog overnight tonight and on Wednesday morning.\nElsewhere, skies will be partly to mostly cloudy. \n\nLow temperatures will range from the lower 40s to lower 50s. \nThe coldest temperatures will be found in central and north-\ncentral Wisconsin.\n\nThursday and Thursday evening\n\nA shortwave trough will move northeast through the Upper\nMississippi River Valley. This trough will be moving into drier\nair and increasing subsidence as it moves into a ridge over the\nGreat Lakes. As a result, the better chances of rain (20-40\npercent) will be west of Highway 52 in northeast Iowa and\nsoutheast Minnesota.\n\nHigh temperatures will be in the mid- to upper 60s. Low\ntemperatures will range from the mid-40s to mid-50s. \n\nFriday into the Weekend\n\nAnother shortwave trough will move slowly northeast through the\nUpper Mississippi River Valley from Friday into the upcoming\nweekend. Like its predecessor, it will be moving into a ridge \nover the Great Lakes. As a result, the models have been \ngradually lowering rainfall amounts and rain chances.\n\nHigh temperatures range from the lower 60s to lower 70s. Low \ntemperatures will range from the mid-40s to mid-50s. \n\nTuesday into Wednesday\n\nA longwave trough will move east through the Mid- and Upper\nMississippi River Valley. This will bring rain into the region.\nThere are some models that bring tropical moisture northward \ninto the region. Meanwhile, other models show that this plume of\ntropical moisture will be southeast of the area.\n\nHigh temperatures will be in the mid- to upper 60s. Low\ntemperatures will range from the mid-40s to mid-50s.",
